## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of demand paging, synchronization, and TLB consistency in a realistic but simple setup.

## Key Concepts
- Demand paging and lazy page-in
- Per-page locking to serialize concurrent faults
- Correct handling of IO vs zero-fill paths
- Page-table updates and TLB invalidation
- Wakeup semantics for waiters

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal fault handler sketch (C-like)
void handle_fault(Page* p){
  acquire(p->lock);
  if (p->present) { release(p->lock); return; }
  if (p->loading) { wait_on(p); release(p->lock); /* queue waiter */ return; }
  p->loading = true; release(p->lock);
  // IO or zero-fill
  if (has_backing_file(p)) read_file_into(p);
  else zero_fill(p);
  acquire(p->lock);
  p->loading = false; p->present = true; update_pte(p);
  flush_tlb(p);
  wake_waiters(p);
  release(p->lock);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test race conditions with two faulting threads? 
- How would you handle I/O errors from the backing file? 
- How would this scale to many pages in parallel?

